        "provenance": "original",
        "title": {
          "en": "The man you cannot see"
        },
        "text": {
          "en": "What would happen if a man could become invisible? H. G. Wells imagines it."
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "explain",
        "type": "explanation",
        "provenance": "original",
        "keepEnglishTerms": [
          "Griffin",
          "a disguise",
          "at Iping",
          "misuse of science",
          "mysterious footprints",
          "problems of being invisible",
          "strange events",
          "the reveal"
        ],
        "text": {
          "en": "Two boys see fresh muddy footprints of bare feet appear with no one there. They follow the prints.\nThe invisible man is Griffin, a scientist who found how to make himself invisible and tried it on himself.\nHe wanders cold and hungry in London, and finds that being invisible brings problems.\nHe goes to a theatrical shop and puts on bandages, dark glasses, a false nose and a hat. He also robs the shopkeeper.\nHe takes a train to the village of Iping and rents two rooms at an inn. People find the stranger odd.\nStrange things happen at the inn: a chair moves and a burglary at the clergyman's house is blamed on him.\nWhen Mrs Hall questions him, he loses his temper, throws off his bandages and nose, and shows he is invisible.\nThe story shows how a scientist can misuse a discovery, and makes us think about invisibility."
        }
